.TH gd_expire 3 "16 March 2026" "Version 0.13.0" "GETDATA"

.SH NAME
gd_expire \(em remove expired chunk files from a Dirfile fragment

.SH SYNOPSIS
.SC
.B #include <getdata.h>
.HP
.BI "int gd_expire(DIRFILE *" dirfile ", off_t " before_frame ,
.BI "int " fragment );
.EC

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.FN gd_expire
function removes chunk files containing data before
.ARG before_frame
from all
.B RAW
fields in the fragment specified by
.ARG fragment
in the dirfile(5) database specified by
.ARG dirfile .
This is intended for implementing a rolling data buffer, where old data is
discarded to free disk space while new data continues to be appended.
.PP
If
.ARG fragment
is the special value
.BR GD_ALL_FRAGMENTS ,
all fragments in the database are processed.
.PP
Only complete chunks whose frame range falls entirely before
.ARG before_frame
are removed.  The chunk containing
.ARG before_frame
is preserved.  The
.ARG before_frame
argument is in the same coordinate space as
.FN gd_getdata
(i.e., it includes the fragment's
.BR /FRAMEOFFSET ).
.PP
The fragment must have a non-zero
.B /CHUNK
size (see
.BR dirfile\-format (5)).
Calling this function on a non-chunked fragment returns
.BR GD_E_UNSUPPORTED .
.PP
After expiry, the
.FN gd_bof
function will return the start of the first remaining chunk for each affected
field.
.PP
Expiry is best-effort: if a chunk file cannot be removed (for example, because
another process has it open), the function continues to the next field rather
than aborting immediately.  All fields are attempted regardless of per-field
failures.  If any chunk files could not be removed,
.B GD_E_IO
is returned after all fields have been processed.  The caller may retry the
operation later, once the obstructing process has released the file.

.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned.  On error, a negative-valued error code is
returned.  Possible error codes are:
.DD GD_E_ACCMODE
The specified dirfile was opened read-only.
.DD GD_E_BAD_DIRFILE
The supplied dirfile was invalid.
.DD GD_E_BAD_INDEX
The supplied fragment index was out of range.
.DD GD_E_IO
An I/O error occurred while attempting to unlink one or more chunk files.
This may indicate that the files are in use by another process.  Expiry of
other fields in the fragment was still attempted.
.DD GD_E_PROTECTED
The binary data of the fragment is protected from change.
.DD GD_E_UNSUPPORTED
The fragment does not use chunked storage (the fragment's
.B /CHUNK
size is zero).
.PP
The error code is also stored in the
.B DIRFILE
object and may be retrieved after this function returns by calling
.F3 gd_error .
A descriptive error string for the error may be obtained by calling
.F3 gd_error_string .

.SH SEE ALSO
.F3 gd_alter_chunk_size ,
.F3 gd_bof ,
.F3 gd_delete ,
.F3 gd_error ,
.F3 gd_error_string ,
.F3 gd_getdata ,
.F3 gd_nframes ,
.F3 gd_open ,
dirfile(5), dirfile-format(5)
